# Monday — Server Components Introduction

[🏠 Index](./00-index.md) · [Tuesday: Dynamic Routes ▶](./02-tuesday-dynamic-routes.md)

## What Are Server Components?

**React Server Components (RSC)** are components that render **entirely on the server**. Their output is HTML (plus a compact serialized description of the UI) — no component code, and none of their dependencies, are shipped to the browser as JavaScript.

This is the foundation of Next.js's **App Router** (the `app/` directory), introduced as the modern alternative to the `pages/` directory.

## App Router vs Pages Router

| | Pages Router (`pages/`) | App Router (`app/`) |
|---|---|---|
| Default component type | Client-rendered (with SSR/SSG helpers) | **Server Component** by default |
| Data fetching | `getStaticProps` / `getServerSideProps` | `async`/`await` directly in the component |
| Layouts | Manual, via `_app.js` | Built-in nested `layout.js` |
| Loading UI | Manual | Built-in `loading.js` |
| Error handling | Manual | Built-in `error.js` |
| Routing folders | File name = route | Folder name = route, `page.js` = the page |

> Both routers can coexist in the same project, but you shouldn't mix them for the same route. This week focuses on the App Router.

## Server vs Client Components

| | Server Component | Client Component |
|---|---|---|
| Where it renders | On the server only | On the server (first paint) *and* in the browser |
| Ships JS to the browser? | No | Yes |
| Can use `useState`, `useEffect`? | No | Yes |
| Can use browser APIs (`window`, `localStorage`)? | No | Yes |
| Can directly access a database / secret API keys? | Yes | No |
| Declared by | Default — no directive needed | `"use client"` at the top of the file |

## File Conventions in the App Router

Each folder inside `app/` maps to a URL segment. Special file names control what renders there:

```
app/
├── layout.js       → shared UI wrapping this segment and its children
├── page.js          → the actual page for this route (makes it public)
├── loading.js        → shown instantly while page.js is loading (Suspense boundary)
├── error.js           → shown if page.js or a child throws
├── not-found.js        → shown for 404s within this segment
└── route.js              → an API endpoint for this segment (like pages/api)
```

## Your First Server Component

```jsx
// app/page.js
export default function HomePage() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Welcome to the App Router</h1>
      <p>This component renders on the server. No "use client" needed.</p>
    </main>
  );
}
```

```jsx
// app/layout.js — required root layout, wraps every page
export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en">
      <body>{children}</body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

## Why Server Components?

- **Smaller JS bundles** — component code and its dependencies never reach the browser.
- **Direct backend access** — query a database or read a file system straight from the component, no API route needed as a middleman.
- **Secrets stay on the server** — API keys and tokens used inside a Server Component are never exposed to the client.
- **Faster initial load** — the server sends ready-made HTML; there's less to hydrate.

## Limitations of Server Components

Server Components **cannot**:

- Use `useState`, `useReducer`, `useEffect`, or any hook that relies on the browser
- Attach event handlers like `onClick` or `onChange`
- Access `window`, `document`, or browser-only storage
- Use browser-only third-party libraries (e.g. libraries built purely for the DOM)

For any of the above, you need a **Client Component** — covered on Thursday.

## Takeaways

- The App Router (`app/`) renders components on the server **by default**.
- Server Components reduce bundle size and can talk to your backend directly.
- Special files (`page.js`, `layout.js`, `loading.js`, `error.js`) give you routing, nesting, loading states, and error boundaries for free.
- Anything interactive or browser-dependent needs `"use client"` — more on that Thursday.

