# Tuesday — Dynamic Routes

[◀ Monday: Server Components Intro](./01-monday-server-components-intro.md) · [🏠 Index](./00-index.md) · [Wednesday: Data Fetching ▶](./03-wednesday-data-fetching-server-components.md)

## Dynamic Segments in the App Router

In the App Router, a dynamic route is created with a **folder** named in square brackets, containing a `page.js`:

```
app/
└── blog/
    └── [slug]/
        └── page.js     → matches /blog/hello-world, /blog/anything
```

## Reading the Param — No `useRouter` Needed

Because `page.js` is a Server Component by default, Next.js just passes the route params to it as a **prop** — no hook required:

```jsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js
export default function BlogPost({ params }) {
  const { slug } = params;

  return (
    <article>
      <h1>Post: {slug}</h1>
    </article>
  );
}
```

## Nested Dynamic Routes

```
app/
└── products/
    └── [category]/
        └── [id]/
            └── page.js   → /products/shoes/42
```

```jsx
// app/products/[category]/[id]/page.js
export default function Product({ params }) {
  const { category, id } = params;
  return (
    <p>Category: {category}, Product ID: {id}</p>
  );
}
```

## Catch-All Segments

```
app/docs/[...slug]/page.js   → matches /docs/a, /docs/a/b, /docs/a/b/c
```

```jsx
// app/docs/[...slug]/page.js
export default function Docs({ params }) {
  const { slug } = params; // array, e.g. ["a", "b", "c"]
  return <p>Path: {slug.join("/")}</p>;
}
```

Optional catch-all (also matches `/docs` itself, with `slug` as `undefined`):

```
app/docs/[[...slug]]/page.js
```

## `generateStaticParams` — Pre-Rendering Dynamic Routes

This replaces `getStaticPaths` from the Pages Router. It tells Next.js which dynamic values to build ahead of time:

```jsx
// app/blog/[slug]/page.js
export async function generateStaticParams() {
  const posts = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts").then((r) => r.json());

  return posts.map((post) => ({
    slug: post.slug,
  }));
}

export default function BlogPost({ params }) {
  return <h1>Post: {params.slug}</h1>;
}
```

Any path *not* returned by `generateStaticParams` is rendered on demand the first time it's requested, then cached — similar to `fallback: "blocking"` in the Pages Router.

## Reading Params in a Client Component

If you need the current params inside a **Client Component** (e.g. for a client-side search box), use the `useParams` hook from `next/navigation` instead of a prop:

```jsx
"use client";
import { useParams } from "next/navigation";

export default function ClientBreadcrumb() {
  const params = useParams();
  return <p>Current slug: {params.slug}</p>;
}
```

## Reading Query Strings

Query strings (`?sort=asc`) are read via the `searchParams` prop on `page.js`, not part of the route path:

```jsx
// app/products/page.js
export default function Products({ searchParams }) {
  const sort = searchParams.sort ?? "default";
  return <p>Sorting by: {sort}</p>;
}
```

## Takeaways

- `[param]` folders create dynamic segments; Server Components receive them via the `params` prop directly.
- `generateStaticParams` pre-builds dynamic pages at build time (replaces `getStaticPaths`).
- `[...slug]` = catch-all, `[[...slug]]` = optional catch-all.
- Use `useParams()` only inside Client Components; Server Components already get `params` as a prop.

