# Wednesday — Data Fetching in Server Components

[◀ Tuesday: Dynamic Routes](./02-tuesday-dynamic-routes.md) · [🏠 Index](./00-index.md) · [Thursday: Client Components ▶](./04-thursday-client-components.md)

## Async Components — `await` Right in the Component

Server Components can be `async` functions. You can `await` data directly in the component body — no `getStaticProps`/`getServerSideProps` wrapper needed:

```jsx
// app/posts/page.js
export default async function Posts() {
  const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");
  const posts = await res.json();

  return (
    <ul>
      {posts.map((post) => (
        <li key={post.id}>{post.title}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

## Extended `fetch()` — Caching Built In

Next.js extends the native `fetch()` with a `cache` option and a `next.revalidate` option, which together replace `getStaticProps`, `getServerSideProps`, and ISR from the Pages Router.

| Fetch option | Behavior | Equivalent to |
|---|---|---|
| `fetch(url)` (default) | Cached indefinitely at build time | `getStaticProps` (SSG) |
| `fetch(url, { cache: "no-store" })` | Re-fetched on every request | `getServerSideProps` (SSR) |
| `fetch(url, { next: { revalidate: 60 } })` | Cached, refreshed at most every 60s | ISR |

```jsx
// SSG-like — cached at build time
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts");

// SSR-like — always fresh
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/live-scores", {
  cache: "no-store",
});

// ISR-like — revalidate every 60 seconds
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/posts", {
  next: { revalidate: 60 },
});
```

## Fetching Directly From a Database

Because Server Components run only on the server, they can talk **directly** to a database or ORM — no API route needed as a middleman:

```jsx
// app/dashboard/page.js
import { db } from "../../lib/db"; // e.g. a Prisma client

export default async function Dashboard() {
  const stats = await db.stat.findMany();

  return (
    <ul>
      {stats.map((s) => (
        <li key={s.id}>{s.label}: {s.value}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

## Parallel vs Sequential Data Fetching

**Sequential** (slower — each `await` blocks the next):

```jsx
export default async function Page() {
  const user = await getUser();       // waits...
  const posts = await getPosts(user.id); // ...then waits again
  return <Profile user={user} posts={posts} />;
}
```

**Parallel** (faster — both requests start at the same time):

```jsx
export default async function Page() {
  const userPromise = getUser();
  const postsPromise = getPosts();

  const [user, posts] = await Promise.all([userPromise, postsPromise]);
  return <Profile user={user} posts={posts} />;
}
```

Use parallel fetching whenever two pieces of data don't depend on each other.

## Streaming With Suspense

Wrap a slow-loading section in `<Suspense>` so the rest of the page can render immediately while that section streams in when ready:

```jsx
import { Suspense } from "react";

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Dashboard</h1>
      <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading stats...</p>}>
        <SlowStats />
      </Suspense>
    </div>
  );
}

async function SlowStats() {
  const stats = await getStatsThatTakeAWhile();
  return <StatsList stats={stats} />;
}
```

A `loading.js` file in a route folder does this automatically for the whole page segment, without manually adding `<Suspense>`.

## Takeaways

- `async`/`await` works directly inside Server Components — no special data-fetching function needed.
- The extended `fetch()` `cache` and `next.revalidate` options give you SSG, SSR, and ISR behavior from one API.
- Server Components can query a database directly, skipping a separate API layer.
- Use `Promise.all` for independent requests; use `Suspense`/`loading.js` to stream slow sections without blocking the whole page.

